DECIDED by the maintainer 2026-08-27: both timeouts everywhere. A bound that
holds on fourteen methods out of eighty-three is not a bound.

What is wrong today, measured by grep over lib/API/Docker/API/:

  read_timeout appears in 5 of 10 resource classes, 33 sites total.
  Networks, Volumes, Secrets, Configs and Distribution forward NEITHER
  timeout from any method. connect_timeout was on the same fourteen
  methods as read_timeout as of #70, and on no others.

So `$docker->networks->list(read_timeout => 5)` returns having ignored the
bound, silently: %opts is a plain hash and a key nothing reads raises
nothing.

The old reasoning was that read_timeout belongs only on the streaming and
long-running methods, because list and inspect return promptly. That is
wrong twice over:

  - "returns promptly" describes a healthy daemon. The whole point of a
    timeout is the unhealthy one. A daemon that accepts the connection and
    then says nothing hangs `list` exactly as it hangs `attach`.
  - connecting is endpoint-independent. Every method opens a socket.

THREE THINGS TO DO

1. Forward both read_timeout and connect_timeout from every public method
   that reaches the transport, in all ten resource classes, and document
   them in each option list. Follow the existing pattern verbatim:

     exists $opts{read_timeout} ? ( read_timeout => $opts{read_timeout} ) : (),

   `exists` and not truth: an explicit 0 turns a client-wide default off
   for one call, and #70's test proves a truth test breaks exactly that.

2. Forward them to the preflight requests too. Three are known:

     containers->attach            the running check via inspect (#53)
     plugins->install / ->upgrade  the /plugins/privileges fetch under
                                   accept_privileges => 1
     API::Docker                   version negotiation, GET /version

   A caller who sets a bound and then hangs in a request they never wrote
   has been told something untrue. Version negotiation is the interesting
   one: it happens before any per-request option exists, so it can only
   take the client attribute -- say so in the POD rather than pretending.

3. Say in the POD what a bound does and does not cover, once, somewhere a
   reader will find it. read_timeout is idle time on reads; connect_timeout
   bounds connecting; neither bounds writing, and SO_RCVTIMEO under TLS
   measures the underlying receives rather than plaintext arrival.

Related: #70 (which forwarded connect_timeout to the fourteen), #61 (which
added it), #59 (read_timeout).
